Assurance Degradation Dynamics (ADD): A Trajectory-Based Formalization of Assurance Quality Under Capability Growth

A Research Proposal for a Theoretical PhD in Frontier AI Safety Assurance

Version 0.5 

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta

Abstract

This proposal investigates whether a trajectory-based formalization of assurance degradation can complement existing frontier-AI safety-case and monitoring methodologies. Assurance Degradation Dynamics (ADD) is presented as a research hypothesis and formalization program—not as an established safety methodology. Unlike static safety cases, ADD models the evolution of assurance quality as a dynamical system coupled to capability growth during training. We define a normalized assurance state space, a log-derivative divergence operator, and an assurance debt variable with explicit repayment dynamics.

Following critical review of v0.4, this version corrects several mathematical, structural, and conceptual errors: (i) Proposition A.1 is rewritten to correctly distinguish functional redundancy from dynamical dependence; (ii) Proposition A.2 is replaced by a structural non-identifiability result for redundant state augmentation; (iii) Theorem A.3 is removed and replaced by an explicit estimator-stability assumption; (iv) the inequality in Appendix B.4 is reversed to the correct direction; (v) the UKF process model is fully specified for latent uncertainty components; (vi) the safety intervention variable is decomposed into effort and effectiveness; (vii) the strategic observation model is clarified with an adversarial bias interpretation; (viii) the MRS-CUSUM detector is properly attributed as an imported result. The framework does not measure safety; it measures the quality and trajectory of our knowledge about safety.

---

1. Problem Statement

1.1 The Capability–Assurance Divergence

Frontier AI development exhibits a structural asymmetry: capability metrics improve monotonically with scale and compute, while assurance mechanisms—evaluation validity, monitorability, and safety-case confidence—do not necessarily improve at the same rate. Wallich and Douglas formalize this as "AI safety debt": the cost of closing the accumulated gaps between an AI system's actual safety approach and the approach it needs, identifying four structural properties that make such debt harder to manage than software technical debt: capabilities and contexts shift unpredictably, closing gaps may require solving open scientific problems, harms largely fall on third parties, and adversaries and AI systems may actively exploit gaps.

Li et al. provide controlled evidence that evaluation awareness varies by model–benchmark pairing rather than by either in isolation, that recognition rarely leads to behavioral change, and that when it does, the direction depends on the type of evaluation perceived. This complicates any monotonic mapping from awareness to degradation.

1.2 The Gap in Existing Formalisms

Existing frameworks are predominantly snapshot-based. Dynamic safety cases have been proposed for frontier AI, emphasizing monitorability, assessability, and updateability. Subjective Logic-based confidence updates integrate windowed Safety Performance Indicators into assurance arguments. Control barrier functions provide provable safety guarantees for dynamical systems, but are designed for control-theoretic safety, not epistemic assurance.

We have not identified, in the literature reviewed to date, a framework that combines trajectory-based assurance quality, capability–assurance divergence, assurance debt dynamics, and dependence-structure monitoring within a frontier-AI training-time architecture.

1.3 Formal Problem Statement

Given a frontier training run \mathcal{T} = \{t_0, t_1, \ldots, t_N\} with observable capability signals C(t) and assurance signals \mathbf{A}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^k, determine whether the trajectory (C(t), \mathbf{A}(t)) exhibits assurance degradation: a state in which the rate of capability growth exceeds the rate of assurance quality improvement such that the system enters a region of irreducible epistemic uncertainty about its own safety properties.

