2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Three-Level Decomposition

We strictly separate three levels of analysis to avoid conflating measurement, inference, and interpretation:

Level 1 — Observation. Raw monitor signals \mathbf{z}(t): what evaluations actually measure.

Level 2 — State Estimation. Filtered estimates \hat{C}(t), \hat{\mathbf{A}}(t), \hat{\mathbf{U}}(t): our estimation of what these observations mean.

Level 3 — Assurance Dynamics. Derived quantities Q_A(t), \mathcal{D}(t), B(t): analytical constructions computed from Levels 1 and 2.

This separation is maintained throughout the architecture and prevents category errors in the theoretical treatment.

2.2 State Space and Process Model

Let the observable state vector be

\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{pmatrix} C(t) \\ \mathbf{A}(t) \\ \mathbf{U}(t) \end{pmatrix} \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^k \times \mathbb{R}^m

where C(t) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} is a scalar capability index, \mathbf{A}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^k is an assurance state vector with components normalized to (0, 1], and \mathbf{U}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^m is an uncertainty state vector with non-negative components.

Process model. The state evolves according to

\mathbf{x}(t+1) = f(\mathbf{x}(t)) + \mathbf{w}(t), \quad \mathbf{w}(t) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{Q})

We specify f componentwise:

C(t+1) = C(t) + \mu_C(t) + w_C(t)

\mathbf{A}(t+1) = \mathbf{A}(t) + \mu_A(t) + \mathbf{w}_A(t)

\mathbf{U}(t+1) = \mathbf{U}(t) + \mu_U(t) + \mathbf{w}_U(t)

where \mu_C(t), \mu_A(t), \mu_U(t) are drift terms estimated online. For latent uncertainty components, the transition model is explicitly:

U_{j}(t+1) = U_j(t) + \eta_j(t), \quad \eta_j(t) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{\eta_j}^2)

This random-walk model is the minimal assumption consistent with the absence of a known mechanistic law. If domain knowledge permits, f_U may be replaced by a more structured model (e.g., Ornstein–Uhlenbeck decay). The drift terms \mu(t) are included as auxiliary states with their own random-walk dynamics, enabling the filter to track systematic growth without treating it as process noise.

Observation model.

\mathbf{z}(t) = \mathbf{H} \mathbf{x}(t) + \mathbf{v}(t), \quad \mathbf{v}(t) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{R})

where \mathbf{H} selects directly observable components. We distinguish:

· Directly measured at step t: A_{\text{monitor}}(t) (chain-of-thought recall), A_{\text{security}}(t) (threat-model audits).
· Latently inferred: U_{\text{eval-validity}}(t) (via EvalAwareBench recognition and behavioral probes), U_{\text{case-defeater}}(t) (severity-weighted defeater counts). These enter \mathbf{H} only when probes are run; otherwise they are estimated via the process model.

Observability condition. For the nonlinear system \mathbf{x}(t) = f(\mathbf{x}(t-1)) + \mathbf{w}(t), \mathbf{z}(t) = h(\mathbf{x}(t)) + \mathbf{v}(t), local observability requires that the nonlinear observability rank condition holds: the observability matrix constructed from the Lie derivatives of h along the vector fields of f must have full column rank. In practice, this imposes a probe scheduling constraint: latent uncertainty components must be probed with sufficient frequency to prevent unobservable subspaces from persisting.

Probe scheduling. We adopt an information-gain-based schedule: a probe for U_j is triggered when the empirical observability matrix \mathcal{O}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}(t)) conditioned on the j-th component falls below a threshold \kappa_{\text{obs}}, or when the trace of the corresponding block of \mathbf{P}(t|t) exceeds a threshold \tau_P. This replaces fixed-interval probing with an adaptive, observability-aware strategy.

2.3 Assurance Quality Functional

Define the assurance quality Q_A(t) as a functional of the assurance and uncertainty states:

Q_A(t) = \mathcal{F}[\mathbf{A}(t), \mathbf{U}(t)]

We require \mathcal{F} to satisfy:

1. Monotonicity in assurance: \partial \mathcal{F} / \partial A_i > 0 for all i.
2. Anti-monotonicity in uncertainty: \partial \mathcal{F} / \partial U_j < 0 for all j.
3. Boundedness: Q_A(t) \in (0, Q_{\max}], with Q_{\max} = 1 under the normalization \sum_i \alpha_i = 1, A_i \leq 1.
4. Conjunctive composition (Axiom A1): Severe degradation in one critical assurance dimension cannot be fully compensated by improvement in unrelated dimensions.

Axiom A1 (Conjunctive Composition). Let \mathbf{A} = (A_1, \ldots, A_k) and suppose A_i \to 0 for some i. Then for any bounded values of A_j, j \neq i,

\lim_{A_i \to 0} \mathcal{F}[\mathbf{A}, \mathbf{U}] = 0

for all \mathbf{U} in a bounded set.

We note explicitly that A1 encodes two distinct assumptions: (i) conjunctivity—severe degradation in one dimension cannot be fully compensated; and (ii) normative weighting—the weights \alpha_i encode a value judgment about the relative importance of dimensions. These are separable hypotheses and should be tested independently.

Primary functional (product form):

Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) = \left( \prod_{i=1}^{k} A_i(t)^{\alpha_i} \right) \cdot \exp\left( -\sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j U_j(t) \right)

with \alpha_i > 0, \sum_i \alpha_i = 1, and \beta_j > 0. This form satisfies A1 and remains strictly positive whenever A_i > 0, preserving the interpretability of the log-derivative g_A.

Bottleneck functional (min form):

Q_A^{\text{min}}(t) = \min_i A_i(t)^{\alpha_i} \cdot \exp\left( -\sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j U_j(t) \right)

Corrected relationship. For A_i \in (0, 1] and \alpha_i > 0,

\boxed{Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) \leq Q_A^{\text{min}}(t)}

Proof. Let x_i = A_i^{\alpha_i} \in (0, 1]. Then \prod_i x_i \leq \min_i x_i since each x_i \leq 1 and the product of terms each \leq 1 is \leq the minimum. Multiplying both sides by the common factor \exp(-\sum_j \beta_j U_j) > 0 preserves the inequality. \square

Consequence. Q_A^{\text{min}} is not a conservative lower bound of Q_A^{\text{prod}}. It is a conservative bottleneck functional that provides a higher value than the weighted product in the region of interest. The robustness rule should be restated accordingly: qualitative disagreement between Q_A^{\text{prod}} and Q_A^{\text{min}} is reported as structural uncertainty arising from the choice of aggregation functional, not as refutation of A1.

Clipping rule. To avoid Q_A = 0, we floor assurance signals: A_i \in [\epsilon_A, 1] with \epsilon_A > 0. Sensitivity to \epsilon_A is reported.

2.4 Normalized Log-Derivative Divergence Operator

Define log-derivative growth rates with regularization \epsilon > 0:

g_C(t) = \frac{d}{dt} \log(C(t) + \epsilon), \quad g_A(t) = \frac{d}{dt} \log(Q_A(t) + \epsilon)

The Capability–Assurance Divergence Operator is

\boxed{\mathcal{D}(t) = g_C(t) - \lambda \, g_A(t)}

where \lambda > 0 is a relative importance parameter. Default: \lambda = 1, with sensitivity analysis across [0.5, 2.0].

Interpretation. \mathcal{D}(t) > 0 indicates that capability is growing proportionally faster than assurance quality.

2.5 Assurance Debt Dynamics

Define assurance debt B(t) \geq 0 satisfying

\boxed{\dot{B}(t) = \max(0, \mathcal{D}(t)) - \gamma \, I_{\text{effort}}(t) \, E_{\text{safety}}(t) \, B(t) + \rho(t)}

with reflection at zero.

Decomposition of intervention. We distinguish:

· Safety effort I_{\text{effort}}(t) \in [0, 1]: resources allocated to safety (fine-tuning compute, red-team budget, monitorability infrastructure).
· Safety effectiveness E_{\text{safety}}(t) \in [0, 1]: empirically observed reduction in assurance degradation per unit of effort.

This decomposition addresses the conceptual gap that effort \neq effectiveness. A laboratory could spend enormously on red teaming and improve practically nothing. The repayment rate is \gamma E_{\text{safety}}(t) I_{\text{effort}}(t).

Operationalization of effort:

I_{\text{effort}}(t) = w_1 \cdot \frac{\text{safety fine-tuning compute}}{\text{total compute}} + w_2 \cdot \frac{\text{red-team budget}}{\text{total budget}} + w_3 \cdot \Delta A_{\text{monitor}}

Operationalization of effectiveness. E_{\text{safety}}(t) is estimated from the observed change in \mathcal{D}(t) following intervention, controlling for capability growth:

E_{\text{safety}}(t) = \mathbb{E}\left[\frac{|\mathcal{D}(t)| - |\mathcal{D}(t-\tau)|}{|\mathcal{D}(t-\tau)|} \,\middle|\, I_{\text{effort}} > 0\right]

estimated via a windowed regression. This is a latent quantity with its own uncertainty, and we acknowledge that its identification requires sufficient intervention events.

Debt as a latent accounting variable. We emphasize:

Assurance debt is a latent accounting variable introduced to represent accumulated assurance deficit under a specified model. Its empirical validity is an open hypothesis.

B(t) is not claimed to be a physical quantity. It is a construction whose usefulness depends on whether it predicts future assurance failures better than simpler alternatives.

Restructuring term. \rho(t) \geq 0 captures debt transferred from resolved to unresolved claims (e.g., closing a monitorability gap by deferring alignment evaluation).

2.6 Assurance Signal Covariance Matrix

Define the assurance signal vector \mathbf{s}(t) = [\mathbf{A}(t); \mathbf{U}(t)] \in \mathbb{R}^{k+m} and the windowed covariance

\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t) = \frac{1}{w-1} \sum_{s=t-w+1}^{t} \left( \mathbf{s}(s) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t) \right) \left( \mathbf{s}(s) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t) \right)^{\top}

where \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t) is the windowed mean and w is the window length.

Structural change detection.

\Delta\Sigma_t = \|\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t-w)\|_F, \quad \Delta R_t = \|\hat{\mathbf{R}}(t) - \hat{\mathbf{R}}(t-w)\|_F

Consistency requirement. We use a single smoothing scale: empirical windowed covariance for \hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t), with \Delta\Sigma_t computed as the Frobenius norm of the windowed difference.

Hypothesis H1 (Structural Early Warning). Dependence-structure monitoring provides earlier detection of selected assurance-degradation scenarios than marginal monitoring alone.

Testable form: Detection latency under covariance monitoring is strictly less than detection latency under marginal monitoring, for scenarios where the hypothesis is designed to hold:

\text{Latency}_{\text{cov}} < \text{Latency}_{\text{marginal}}

Detection framework. We use an MRS-CUSUM-inspired detector, building on the Multi-Rank Subspace-CUSUM procedure of Lee et al., which extends classical CUSUM by tracking projection energy onto an estimated signal subspace and achieves first-order asymptotic optimality relative to the oracle Exact CUSUM under a spiked covariance model. We explicitly label our implementation MRS-C-inspired until exact replication of the algorithm and its assumptions is achieved.

