Appendix B: Functional Selection and Axiom A1

B.1 Axiomatic Foundations

Axiom A1 (Conjunctive Composition). Severe degradation in one critical assurance dimension cannot be fully compensated by improvement in unrelated dimensions.

Formally, let \mathbf{A} = (A_1, \ldots, A_k) and suppose A_i \to 0 for some i. Then for any bounded values of A_j, j \neq i,

\lim_{A_i \to 0} \mathcal{F}[\mathbf{A}, \mathbf{U}] = 0

for all \mathbf{U} in a bounded set.

Remark B.1.1. Axiom A1 encodes two distinct assumptions: (i) conjunctivity — severe degradation in one dimension cannot be fully compensated; and (ii) normative weighting — the weights \alpha_i encode a value judgment about the relative importance of dimensions. These are separable hypotheses and should be tested independently.

B.2 Product Functional

Proposition B.1. The product functional

Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) = \left( \prod_{i=1}^{k} A_i(t)^{\alpha_i} \right) \cdot \exp\left( -\sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j U_j(t) \right)

satisfies Axiom A1.

Proof. If A_i \to 0 for some i and \alpha_i > 0, then A_i^{\alpha_i} \to 0. Since all other factors are bounded and positive, the product tends to zero. \square

Proposition B.2. Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) > 0 for all t if A_i(t) > 0 for all i.

Proof. Each A_i^{\alpha_i} > 0 and \exp(-\sum_j \beta_j U_j) > 0. The product of positive terms is positive. \square

B.3 Weighted-Sum Functional Violates A1

Proposition B.3. The weighted-sum functional

Q_A^{\text{weighted}} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \alpha_i A_i - \sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j U_j

violates Axiom A1.

Proof. Suppose A_1 \to 0 and A_2 \to \infty with \alpha_2 > 0. Then Q_A^{\text{weighted}} \to \infty, contradicting A1. \square

B.4 Bottleneck Functional — Corrected Inequality

Proposition B.4. For any \mathbf{A} \in (0, 1]^k,

\boxed{Q_A^{\text{prod}}(t) \leq Q_A^{\text{min}}(t)}

Proof. Let x_i = A_i^{\alpha_i} \in (0, 1]. Then \prod_i x_i \leq \min_i x_i since each x_i \leq 1 and the product of terms each \leq 1 is \leq the minimum. Multiplying both sides by the common factor \exp(-\sum_j \beta_j U_j) > 0 preserves the inequality. \square

Remark B.4.1. Q_A^{\text{min}} is not a conservative lower bound of Q_A^{\text{prod}}. It is a conservative bottleneck functional that provides a higher value in the region of interest. The original v0.3 claim that Q_A^{\text{min}} \leq Q_A^{\text{prod}} was incorrect. Example: A_1 = A_2 = 0.5, \alpha_1 = \alpha_2 = 1 gives Q_{\text{prod}} = 0.25 while Q_{\text{min}} = 0.5.

B.5 Robustness Rule

Definition B.5 (Functional Robustness). Let \mathcal{C} be a conclusion derived from Q_A^{\text{prod}} and \mathcal{C}' the corresponding conclusion from Q_A^{\text{min}}. If \mathcal{C} and \mathcal{C}' differ qualitatively (e.g., sign of \mathcal{D} changes), this disagreement is reported as structural uncertainty arising from the choice of aggregation functional.

