Appendix C: Proofs of Theoretical Results

C.1 Lemma 1 (Debt Monotonicity — Definitional Consequence)

Lemma 1. If \mathcal{D}(t) > 0 for all t \in [t_1, t_2], I_{\text{effort}}(t) E_{\text{safety}}(t) = 0 for all t \in [t_1, t_2], and B(t) is continuous, then B(t) is strictly increasing on [t_1, t_2].

Proof. From the debt dynamics,

\dot{B}(t) = \max(0, \mathcal{D}(t)) - \gamma \cdot 0 \cdot B(t) = \mathcal{D}(t) > 0

Since \dot{B}(t) > 0 on [t_1, t_2] and B is continuous, for any t_1 \leq s < t < t_2,

B(t) - B(s) = \int_s^t \dot{B}(\tau) \, d\tau > 0

Therefore B(t_2) > B(t_1). \square

Remark C.1.1. This is a definitional consequence of the debt model, not a mathematical discovery. It confirms internal consistency. The assumptions B(t) \geq 0 and continuity are required for strict increase; without them, reflection at zero could produce a non-strict increase.

C.2 Proposition 2 (Boundedness Under Intervention — Corrected with Restructuring)

Proposition 2. Suppose |\mathcal{D}(t)| \leq D_{\max} and \rho(t) \leq \rho_{\max} for all t. If there exists \delta > 0 and T > 0 such that I_{\text{effort}}(t) E_{\text{safety}}(t) \geq \delta for all t \geq T, then

\limsup_{t \to \infty} B(t) \leq \frac{D_{\max} + \rho_{\max}}{\gamma \delta}

and B(t) is eventually contained in the invariant set [0, (D_{\max}+\rho_{\max})/(\gamma\delta)].

Proof. For t \geq T,

\dot{B}(t) = \max(0, \mathcal{D}(t)) - \gamma I_{\text{effort}}(t) E_{\text{safety}}(t) B(t) + \rho(t) \leq D_{\max} - \gamma \delta B(t) + \rho_{\max}

Let u(t) = B(t) - (D_{\max}+\rho_{\max})/(\gamma\delta). Then

\dot{u}(t) \leq -\gamma \delta u(t)

By the standard differential inequality comparison / Gronwall argument (Grönwall, 1919; see also Wikipedia, "Grönwall's inequality"),

u(t) \leq u(T) e^{-\gamma\delta(t-T)}

Therefore,

B(t) \leq B(T) e^{-\gamma\delta(t-T)} + \frac{D_{\max}+\rho_{\max}}{\gamma\delta} \left(1 - e^{-\gamma\delta(t-T)}\right)

As t \to \infty, the right-hand side tends to (D_{\max}+\rho_{\max})/(\gamma\delta). Since B(t) \geq 0 by definition, the invariant set is [0, (D_{\max}+\rho_{\max})/(\gamma\delta)]. \square

Remark C.2.1. The v0.3 proof incorrectly claimed B(t) \to D_{\max}/(\gamma\delta). This is false: if \mathcal{D}(t) = 0 and I_{\text{effort}} E_{\text{safety}} = \delta, then \dot{B} = -\gamma\delta B, so B(t) \to 0. The correct statement is an upper bound, not convergence.

Remark C.2.2. If \rho(t) is unbounded or persistently positive without bound, no finite upper bound exists. The bound requires \rho_{\max} < \infty.

C.3 Corollary (Lipschitz Bound)

Corollary C.3. If \lambda = 1 and both C(t) and Q_A(t) are Lipschitz-continuous with constants L_C and L_Q, then

D_{\max} \leq \frac{L_C}{C_{\min} + \epsilon} + \frac{L_Q}{Q_{\min} + \epsilon}

Proof. The log-derivative is

g_C(t) = \frac{d}{dt} \log(C(t) + \epsilon) = \frac{\dot{C}(t)}{C(t) + \epsilon}

Since |\dot{C}(t)| \leq L_C and C(t) \geq C_{\min},

|g_C(t)| \leq \frac{L_C}{C_{\min} + \epsilon}

Similarly, |g_A(t)| \leq L_Q/(Q_{\min} + \epsilon). Therefore,

D_{\max} = |g_C - g_A| \leq |g_C| + |g_A| \leq \frac{L_C}{C_{\min} + \epsilon} + \frac{L_Q}{Q_{\min} + \epsilon}

\square

C.4 Proposition 3 (Covariance Structure Change — Corrected)

Proposition 3. Let \rho(t) = \operatorname{Corr}(A_{\text{monitor}}(t), A_{\text{align}}(t)) and suppose the standard deviations \sigma_1(t), \sigma_2(t) are constant over the window [t-w, t]: \sigma_1(t) = \sigma_1, \sigma_2(t) = \sigma_2. If the correlation changes by \Delta\rho = \rho(t) - \rho(t-w), then

\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t) - \boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t-w)\|_F \geq \sqrt{2} \, \sigma_1 \sigma_2 |\Delta\rho|

Proof. The off-diagonal covariance entry is \operatorname{Cov}(t) = \sigma_1 \sigma_2 \rho(t). Under constant variances,

|\Delta \operatorname{Cov}| = |\sigma_1 \sigma_2 \rho(t) - \sigma_1 \sigma_2 \rho(t-w)| = \sigma_1 \sigma_2 |\Delta\rho|

The Frobenius norm includes this off-diagonal entry twice (at positions (1,2) and (2,1)), giving

\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t) - \boldsymbol{\Sigma}(t-w)\|_F^2 \geq 2 (\Delta \operatorname{Cov})^2 = 2 \sigma_1^2 \sigma_2^2 (\Delta\rho)^2

Taking square roots yields the stated bound. \square

Remark C.4.1. The v0.3 bound lacked the \sqrt{2} factor. The v0.3 proof also assumed constant variances without stating it. Without this assumption, \Delta \operatorname{Cov} = \sigma_1(t)\sigma_2(t)\rho(t) - \sigma_1(t-w)\sigma_2(t-w)\rho(t-w), and the simple lower bound fails. The proposition is a detection statistic, not a deterministic early-warning theorem.

C.5 MRS-CUSUM Detection — Imported Result

Proposition C.5 (Imported Result). Under the assumptions of Lee et al. (2025), the MRS-C procedure possesses the asymptotic efficiency property established in that work: it is first-order asymptotically optimal relative to the oracle Exact CUSUM, with an explicit efficiency constant depending on heterogeneity in spike strengths.

Attribution. This result is not claimed as a new theorem of ADD. It is imported from Lee et al. (2025), "Multi-Rank Subspace Change-Point Detection with Application in Monitoring Robotic Swarms," arXiv:2506.18562. The authors prove that MRS-C is first-order asymptotically optimal relative to the oracle Exact CUSUM, with an explicit efficiency constant that depends on heterogeneity in spike strengths. When the signal rank is unknown, they use a parallel procedure.

Remark C.5.1. Our implementation is labeled MRS-C-inspired until exact replication of the algorithm and its assumptions is achieved. The optimality theorem is not transferred to ADD as a new result.

