Appendix E: Covariance Estimation and MRS-CUSUM Detection

E.1 Windowed Covariance Estimation

Let \mathbf{s}(t) = [\mathbf{A}(t); \mathbf{U}(t)] \in \mathbb{R}^{k+m}. The windowed covariance is

\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t) = \frac{1}{w-1} \sum_{s=t-w+1}^{t} \left(\mathbf{s}(s) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t)\right)\left(\mathbf{s}(s) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t)\right)^{\top}

where \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}(t) = \frac{1}{w} \sum_{s=t-w+1}^{t} \mathbf{s}(s).

E.2 Structural Change Statistic

\Delta\Sigma_t = \|\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t) - \hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t-w)\|_F

E.3 Spiked Covariance Model

Under the spiked covariance model, the covariance matrix has the form

\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \mathbf{I} + \sum_{j=1}^{r} \lambda_j \mathbf{v}_j \mathbf{v}_j^{\top}

where \lambda_1 \geq \cdots \geq \lambda_r > 0 are spike strengths and \mathbf{v}_j are orthonormal eigenvectors. The spiked covariance model provides a tractable framework for capturing low-dimensional changes in covariance.

Hierarchy of models.

· Theoretical model: Spiked covariance + independence, for analytical results.
· Experimental model: Dependent, non-stationary data, evaluated empirically.
· Robustness: Test Student-t, block bootstrap, and non-Gaussian distributions.

E.4 MRS-CUSUM Statistic

Let \hat{\mathbf{U}}_d(t) be the matrix of top d principal directions of \hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}(t). The projection energy is

E_t = \|\hat{\mathbf{U}}_d(t)^{\top} \mathbf{s}(t)\|^2

The MRS-C statistic is

S_t = \max\left(0, S_{t-1} + E_t - \kappa\right)

with alarm when S_t > h.

E.5 Threshold Calibration

The threshold h is calibrated to achieve a prescribed average run length \text{ARL}_0 under the null hypothesis. For large \text{ARL}_0, the approximation is

h \approx \frac{\log(\text{ARL}_0)}{\kappa}

where \kappa is the drift parameter. The subspace dimension d is selected via a parallel procedure when the signal rank is unknown, following Lee et al. (2025).

