Appendix F: Strategic Observation and Robust Filtering

F.1 Gaming Model — Bias Interpretation

We model strategic observation as a bias in the observation model:

\mathbf{z}(t) = \mathbf{H} \mathbf{x}(t) + \mathbf{b}_{\text{game}}(t) + \mathbf{v}(t)

where \mathbf{b}_{\text{game}}(t) represents adversarial inflation of assurance signals. When I_{\text{effort}}(t) > 0, the model may increase apparent A_i or decrease apparent U_j. We parameterize:

\mathbf{b}_{\text{game}}(t) = \kappa_g \cdot I_{\text{effort}}(t) \cdot \left(1 - e^{-t/\tau_g}\right) \cdot \mathbf{d}

where \mathbf{d} is a direction vector capturing which signals are inflated, \kappa_g > 0, and \tau_g > 0. This is a bias, not additional state uncertainty. This represents adversarial inflation of assurance signals, not additional state uncertainty.

F.2 Robust Filtering

Switch from the standard UKF to an adaptive UKF with inflated process noise when I_{\text{effort}} is high:

\mathbf{Q}_{\text{adaptive}}(t) = \mathbf{Q}_0 + \eta \cdot I_{\text{effort}}(t) \cdot \mathbf{I}

where \eta > 0 is a robustness parameter.

F.3 Behavioral Probes

Statistical innovation monitoring catches anomalies but may miss smooth gaming that produces white innovations. We add periodic held-out adversarial evaluations using Trusted Debate protocols, where two trusted agents argue opposing views about code safety under a judge's evaluation, to expose hidden vulnerabilities. These provide additional evidence against strategic observation, not definitive detection.

